Module 14: Routing Concepts and Module 15: IP Static Routing

This part of my CCNA learning journey focuses on routing and how routers move packets between different networks. I am working through the concepts in Cisco's networking curriculum and putting what I learn into practice using Cisco Packet Tracer.

The aim is to understand how routers make forwarding decisions, how routing tables work, and how to configure static routes for different networking scenarios. I am documenting my progress, practical exercises, configuration commands, challenges, and lessons learnt along the way.

Module 14: Routing Concepts

14.0 Introduction

An introduction to routing concepts and the role routers play in moving data between networks.

14.1 Path Determination

Understanding how routers determine the best path for forwarding packets towards their destinations.

14.1.1 Two Functions of a Router: Understanding the router's role in selecting paths and forwarding packets.

14.1.2 Router Functions Example: Exploring how a router processes packets and makes forwarding decisions.

14.1.3 Best Path Equals Longest Match: Understanding how longest-prefix matching helps a router select the most specific matching route.

14.1.4 IPv4 Address Longest Match Example: Applying longest-prefix matching to IPv4 routing entries.

14.1.5 IPv6 Address Longest Match Example: Applying longest-prefix matching to IPv6 routing entries.

14.1.6 Build the Routing Table: Understanding how routers learn and maintain routes to destination networks.

14.1.7 Check Your Understanding: Path Determination: Reviewing the key concepts involved in path selection.

14.2 Packet Forwarding

Exploring how routers receive packets, examine destination addresses, consult routing information, and forward packets towards their destinations.

14.3 Basic Router Configuration Review

Revisiting basic router configuration, interface addressing, interface activation, and verification commands.

14.4 IP Routing Table

Learning how to read and interpret routing tables, identify route sources, and understand how routers use routing entries to make forwarding decisions.

14.5 Static and Dynamic Routing

Comparing manually configured static routes with routes learned through dynamic routing protocols, including their differences, advantages, and practical uses.

14.6 Module Practice and Quiz

Reviewing the module content and testing my understanding of routing concepts, path determination, packet forwarding, and routing tables.

Module 15: IP Static Routing

15.1 Static Routes

Understanding static routing, how static routes work, and when manually configured routes are useful.

15.2 Configure IP Static Routes

Practising the configuration of static routes to direct traffic towards specific destination networks.

15.3 Configure IP Default Static Routes

Learning how to configure a default static route for destinations that do not have a more specific matching route in the routing table.

15.4 Configure Floating Static Routes

Configuring backup static routes with a higher administrative distance so they can be used when the preferred route becomes unavailable.

15.5 Configure Static Host Routes

Learning how to configure routes for individual destination host addresses rather than entire networks.

15.6 Module Practice and Quiz

Reviewing static routing concepts, practising configuration commands, verifying route selection, and testing connectivity.

Practical Labs and Documentation

Throughout these modules, I use Cisco Packet Tracer to reinforce the concepts through practical network configurations and troubleshooting exercises.

Where applicable, I document:

Network topology and addressing schemes.

Router interface and routing configurations.

Static, default, floating, and host route configuration.

Routing table inspection and verification commands.

Connectivity testing and troubleshooting.

Configuration errors encountered and how I resolved them.

Key lessons learnt from each exercise.

Learning Objectives

By the end of these modules, my aim is to be able to:

Explain how routers determine the best path to a destination.

Apply longest-prefix matching to IPv4 and IPv6 routing scenarios.

Interpret routing tables and understand route selection.

Explain the packet-forwarding process.

Distinguish between static and dynamic routing.

Configure and verify static routes on Cisco routers.

Configure default, floating, and static host routes.

Troubleshoot basic routing and connectivity problems.

